<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad IX of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad al-Aysar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad IX, called al-Aysar (the Left-handed), was Nasrid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> four times between 1419 and 1453. Supported by the Abencerraje family, he was repeatedly deposed and restored in civil wars with rival claimants, some backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, such as Yusuf IV in 1432. His long, interrupted rule coincided with Castilian advances and deepened the divisions that weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ix-of-granada.jpg" alt="Muhammad IX of Granada" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hispalois · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAN_Hispalois_dobla_MuhammadIX_Granada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad IX, known in Castilian as El Zurdo, was a grandson of Muhammad V and a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ii">Yusuf II</a>. He was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> around 1384, the year his grandfather held his circumcision ceremony, and in his youth he was imprisoned at Salobreña, probably after Muhammad VIII came to the throne. Of his two known wives, Zahr al-Riyad and Umm al-Fath, the second was a frequent adviser on matters of state. He had no son, and his three daughters married Muhammad X, possibly Yusuf V, and Abu l-Hasan Ali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Provincial governors and the Banu al-Sarraj freed him in 1419 and proclaimed him sultan, after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> declared the eight-year-old Muhammad VIII unfit to rule. Taking the epithet al-Galib bi-llah, he made Abu al-Hajjaj Yusuf ibn al-Sarraj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, kept truces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile</a> and courted the Hafsid ruler Abu Faris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, who sheltered him when he was overthrown in January 1427. He returned in 1429 and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> in March 1430, then had Muhammad VIII executed. After the Castilian victory at La Higueruela on 1 July 1431 he lost the throne to Yusuf IV, but his supporters took the Alhambra back in 1432.</p><p>His third reign brought frontier raids, the loss of Huelma in 1438 and a truce in 1439; he refused formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Faced with the revolt of his nephew Yusuf V he abdicated in 1445, but returned in 1447 after Yusuf's fall. In 1448 his forces defeated Juan de Saavedra at Río Verde near Marbella, a battle remembered in Castilian ballads, and in 1450 he retook Málaga from the pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-iii">Ismail III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Granadan commanders died in the defeat at Alporchones in 1452, and he agreed a five-year truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1 September 1452. He died in July 1453, aged more than sixty-nine by the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>'s official letter. With about 28 years on the throne he ruled longest of the 15th-century sultans, though he never had wide popular support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad IX of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
